Actually, let me reconsider. The description says the crash is a "Stack-buffer-overflow READ". The fix is in `reframe_latm.c`. Let me look at the `latm_dmx_process` function again:

```c
u8 latm_buffer[4096];
u32 latm_frame_size = 4096;
if (!latm_dmx_sync_frame_bs(ctx->bs,&ctx->acfg, &latm_frame_size, latm_buffer, NULL)) break;
```

In `latm_dmx_sync_frame_bs`:
```c
size = 0;
while (1) {
    u32 tmp = gf_bs_read_int(bs, 8);
    size += tmp;
    if (tmp!=255) break;
}
if (gf_bs_available(bs) < size) {
    gf_bs_seek(bs, pos-3);
    return GF_FALSE;
}
if (nb_bytes) {
    *nb_bytes = (u32) size;
}
if (buffer) {
    gf_bs_read_data(bs, (char *) buffer, size);
}
```

So `size` can be up to `gf_bs_available(bs)`. If the input is large enough, `size` can exceed 4096, and `gf_bs_read_data(bs, (char *) buffer, size)` writes `size` bytes into the 4096-byte stack buffer → stack-buffer-overflow WRITE.

But the description says READ. Hmm. Let me reconsider.

Actually, maybe the crash is a READ because of the `memcpy(output, latm_buffer, latm_frame_size)` in `latm_dmx_process`. If `latm_frame_size` (which is `size`) is larger than 4096, then `memcpy` reads `latm_frame_size` bytes from `latm_buffer` (which is only 4096 bytes) → stack-buffer-overflow READ.

But wait, `gf_bs_read_data` would have already written `size` bytes into `latm_buffer`, overflowing it. So the WRITE would be detected first by ASAN.

Unless... the `gf_bs_read_data` doesn't actually write all `size` bytes because the bitstream is at the end. Let me look at `gf_bs_read_data`.

Actually, let me reconsider. Maybe the crash is a READ because `gf_bs_read_data` reads from the input buffer beyond its bounds. Let me look at `gf_bs_read_data`.

